## Prerequisites
- GitHub Copilot subscription (Individual, Business, or Enterprise)
- Node.js 20+ (for the TypeScript SDK path)
- `gh` CLI installed and authenticated

## Install the GitHub Copilot SDK (TypeScript)

```bash
npm install @github/copilot-sdk
```

## Create a minimal Copilot extension (tool)

```typescript
// my-tool.ts
import { defineTool } from '@github/copilot-sdk';

export const myDeployTool = defineTool({
  name: 'trigger-deploy',
  description: 'Trigger a staging deployment for the current branch',
  parameters: {
    type: 'object',
    properties: {
      environment: {
        type: 'string',
        enum: ['staging', 'canary'],
        description: 'Target deploy environment',
      },
    },
    required: ['environment'],
  },
  async execute({ environment }) {
    // Your deployment logic here
    const result = await fetch(`https://deploy.internal/api/${environment}`, {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.DEPLOY_TOKEN}` },
    });
    return { success: result.ok, status: result.status };
  },
});
```

## Register and run your extension locally

```bash
# Build
npx tsc

# Register with the Copilot app (local dev mode)
gh copilot extension register --tool ./dist/my-tool.js --dev

# The extension now shows up in the Copilot app's tool picker
# Restart the Copilot desktop app if it was already running
```

## Gotcha
The SDK's `defineTool` schema uses JSON Schema draft-07. Copilot agents will silently skip tools whose parameter schemas use draft-2020 features (e.g. `prefixItems`). Stick to `type`, `properties`, `required`, and `enum` for maximum compatibility in the current preview.
